Package: bwebs
Title: Bayesian Food Webs from Compiled Trophic Interactions
Version: 0.4.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Pablo", "Amador Crespo", email = "pamadorc@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-3793-8794")),
    person("Andreu", "Rico", role = "aut",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-1820-4218"))
  )
Description: Infers the potential trophic links among the taxa of a
    community from compilations of published interactions, with the method
    of Gray et al. (2015) <doi:10.1016/j.fooweb.2015.09.001> and its
    registry of freshwater interactions, estimates with a Bayesian
    hierarchical model how often links inferred with each class of taxonomic
    evidence are observed in independent food webs, compares matching rules
    and sources of interactions (that registry, Global Biotic Interactions
    and the freshwater food webs of GATEWAy, Brose et al. 2019
    <doi:10.1038/s41559-019-0899-x>) by cross-validated Brier scores, gives
    the pairs without an inferred link a probability that decays with the
    taxonomic distance of their closest records, lowers the probability of
    predation between taxa of implausible body sizes,
    samples plausible food webs from the link probabilities for every sample
    of an abundance or biomass table, summarises standard topological
    indicators with credible intervals and computes the energy fluxes of
    every realization with the food-web energetics approach of 'fluxweb'
    (Gauzens et al. 2019 <doi:10.1111/2041-210X.13109>) from the biomasses
    and body masses of the same draw.
    Includes the Tuesday Lake food webs (Cohen et al. 2003
    <doi:10.1073/pnas.232715699>) as an independent test.
